<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Permagent uses the microphone for voice input to the assistant, and to capture your side of a meeting when you start a recording.</string>

	<!-- Video calls in the in-app browser (Zoom / Google Meet / Teams).
	     macOS does not merely DENY camera access when this key is absent — it
	     terminates the process the moment capture is requested. Joining a call
	     in the Build-tab browser would therefore have killed the app rather
	     than showing a permission prompt. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Permagent uses the camera for video calls you join in the in-app browser.</string>

	<!-- The Calendar card reads today's events by sending Apple events to
	     Calendar.app (`osascript` + `tell application "Calendar"`, in
	     goose-server's dashboard_cards route). That is Automation, NOT the
	     EventKit calendar permission, so the key macOS looks for is
	     NSAppleEventsUsageDescription — NSCalendarsUsageDescription would do
	     nothing here because nothing in the app links EventKit.

	     Without this key macOS refuses the event with -1743 and never shows a
	     prompt, which is why the card could only ever say "Grant Calendar
	     access" with no way to grant it.

	     IMPORTANT, and the reason this key alone does not finish the job: TCC
	     attributes the request to the RESPONSIBLE process, which is whatever
	     started `permagentd`. This Info.plist only covers the case where the
	     app itself spawned the daemon as a bundled sidecar (daemon.rs mode 3).
	     When launchd owns permagentd (mode 2, the normal installed setup) the
	     responsible process is a bare executable with no Info.plist at all,
	     and no prompt is possible no matter what this file says. -->
	<key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Permagent reads today's events from your Apple Calendar to show them on the Home dashboard.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
